<?xml version="1.0"?>
<sdf version="1.9">
  <!-- Section A shelf: three slots on an arc at radius 0.30 m, tops at z = 0.100,
       each with a pair of CHAMFERED anti-roll barriers, plus two perpendicular walls
       the LiDAR uses as an absolute pose reference.

       HEIGHT 0.100, was 0.170 (2026-09-28). Lowered to the height the reorient section is
       verified to stand objects up on, so the two sections share one platform height and
       the combined task - pick from the floor, stand it up, place it on the shelf - can use
       this shelf as it is. Every other dimension below is relative to the top surface and
       is unchanged; only the heights moved down 70 mm.
       NOTE ON "PEDESTALS". The three pedestal boxes are 0.32 m long and their centres
       are 0.2536 m apart, so they OVERLAP: the shelf is one continuous arc-shaped mass,
       not three islands. Earlier comments here described them as separate, which is
       wrong and is what made the barrier overhang between slots easy to miss.

       RADIAL WIDTH 0.18, NOT 0.16. At 0.16 the pedestal edge was at radial 0.080
       and the barricade's lower outer corner was also at 0.0800 - exactly flush, zero
       margin. That reads visually as the barrier hanging off the rim, and it leaves an
       object that overshoots the crest nothing to land on but air. At 0.18 there is
       10 mm of solid shelf outside the barricade. The pedestal now spans radius 0.21 to
       0.39, still 32 mm clear of the robot's 0.1775 m base and 60 mm clear of the back
       wall.

       PEDESTALS ON AN ARC, NOT ONE SLAB. The robot has a fixed base and places at a
       0.30 m reach, so its slots lie on an arc. Objects are placed LYING with their
       long axis tangential, so adjacent slots need at least 0.25 m between centres -
       50 deg of azimuth. A single rectangular slab wide enough to carry all three would
       have to reach x = -0.105, and the robot's base occupies everything inside a
       0.1775 m radius; it would intersect the robot. Pedestals following the arc do
       not. Each is 0.32 m along the object and 0.16 m across, giving 80 mm of margin
       on the longest object - the first version left only 20 mm and any heading error
       left the object overhanging.

       ======================================================================
       CHAMFERED ANTI-ROLL BARRIERS - the second attempt, and why this one works
       ======================================================================
       A cylinder on a flat top rolls off at the slightest disturbance; in the first
       run the bottle rolled off the shelf and landed on the robot's own base. So a
       barrier is needed. The FIRST attempt was a plain rectangular rail, and it
       created a worse failure than the one it fixed: it presented a horizontal top
       face with a square edge inside the region the object is lowered into, the object
       landed on that face instead of the pedestal, and the arm - which lowers to where
       it believes the surface is - went on pressing a rigid object against a rigid
       obstruction until the reaction tipped the whole robot onto its casters.

       The lesson was not "no barriers". It was that a barrier must not present any
       horizontal surface, or any square edge, that a descending object can rest on.
       So each barrier here is a RAMP: its only exposed surface is a 35 deg chamfer
       rising away from the slot centre. An object that lands off-centre touches an
       inclined plane, and an inclined plane converts the arm's downward motion into a
       sideways push that drives the object into the slot. The failure mode becomes the
       correction mechanism.

       Every number below is derived, and all of them are re-checked by
       test_shelf_barriers_are_properly_chamfered in the behaviour test suite.

         chamfer angle   35 deg from horizontal.
                         Steep enough that the object always slides back DOWN rather
                         than sticking on the slope: sliding needs mu < tan(35) = 0.70
                         and the ramp faces are set to mu = 0.30. It is also shallow
                         enough to give a wide capture mouth for a given crest height,
                         which a steep chamfer cannot.
         foot   r=0.056  where the chamfer meets the pedestal surface. The object's
                         half-width is 0.045, so a correctly placed object sits in an
                         11 mm gap either side and touches nothing.
         crest  r=0.0765 3.5 mm inside the pedestal edge at 0.080. The mouth is
                z=0.1143 therefore 0.0765 - 0.045 = 31.5 mm wider than the object on
                         each side: any radial placement error up to 31.5 mm is funnelled
                         in. Measured localisation error is 2.7 mm, so this is a factor
                         of ten of margin rather than a hopeful guess.
         height 14.3 mm  Escaping the slot means lifting the object's centre of mass by
                         the full crest height. For the bottle that is
                         0.45 x 9.81 x 0.0143 = 63 mJ, while a brisk 0.15 m/s roll
                         carries only 7.6 mJ - a factor of eight. So the crest can be
                         this low, and being low is what keeps it clear of the hands.
         plate  0.025 x  Modelled as a thin plate rotated by -35 deg about the
                0.300 x  tangential axis. Its lower end is buried 1 mm into the
                0.006    pedestal so that the chamfer runs into the surface with no
                         step: a gap there would simply be a new square edge to catch
                         on, which is the whole failure being fixed. Length 0.300
                         against the pedestal's 0.320 leaves the ends inset 10 mm.

       LENGTH 0.260, AND THIS IS THE DIMENSION THAT MATTERED MOST. The barriers were
       0.300 long, which put them 3.6 mm INSIDE the neighbouring slot's landing zone,
       standing 9.4 mm above the surface. Adjacent slot centres are only 0.2536 m apart
       (50 deg at a 0.30 m radius), so a barrier running the full length of its own slot
       necessarily sweeps across its neighbour's. An object lowered into slot 2 would
       land on slot 1's barrier - and worse than landing on its own, because that
       barrier's chamfer is oriented for slot 1 and pushes the object the WRONG WAY,
       away from the slot it is meant to be entering. This was the remaining cause of
       the arm pressing down on an obstructed object during placement.
       At 0.260 the nearest above-surface corner clears the neighbouring footprint by
       11.7 mm while still running 10 mm past each end of the 0.240 m bottle it has to
       cradle. 0.290 is where intrusion begins, so this is not a dimension to trim.
       Checked by test_barriers_do_not_reach_into_another_slot, which works in WORLD
       coordinates - the per-slot chamfer test cannot see this fault, because every
       quantity it examines is local to one slot.

       WHY THE ENDS NEED NO CHAMFER. The object descends vertically, so the only
       surfaces it can strike are those with upward-facing area in its footprint. Each
       barrier's end faces are vertical, 14 mm tall, and lie at radial 0.056 to 0.0765 -
       entirely outside the object's +/-0.045 footprint. They cannot be landed on. The
       one surface the object can reach is the chamfer, and that is inclined everywhere.

       WHY THE HANDS NEVER TOUCH THEM. The grasp point sits 0.025 m above the object's
       centre, 0.170 at the lowest point of a placement, and the fingertips - with the distal
       phalanx - reach 0.062 below it, to z = 0.108: 6 mm BELOW the 0.1143 crest. So the
       separation is RADIAL: closed, the fingers' outer faces sit at 0.046 (cylinders, tips
       curled inward) or 0.054 (the box) from the slot line, inboard of where the chamfer's
       surface starts (0.060 - the 0.056 foot is its buried lower corner), and when the
       hands let go they open only to 4 mm off the object (motion.release_closure), outer
       face at 0.061, where the chamfer is 7 mm below the straight tips. Opened FULLY there,
       the outer face would be at 0.071 with 0.3 mm to spare - no margin for tracking
       error - so the hands rise clear first and open fully in the air. The first
       attempt at barriers was positioned by finger reasoning alone and still failed,
       because clearing the FINGERS was never the problem - clearing the OBJECT was.

       WHY THE WELD MUST BE RELEASED FIRST. A chamfer can only push an object that is
       free to move. While the DetachableJoint weld is active the object is rigidly part
       of the arm and a sideways force just fights the position controller - exactly the
       tipping failure again. So the placement descends only to 2 mm above the crest,
       releases the weld there, and only then settles the last 16 mm with the fingers
       still lightly closed, so the object can slide within the grip and seat itself.
       See place_waypoints / settle_waypoints in choreography.py.

       FLAT BACK WALL - the absolute heading reference. Section A's base only rotates,
       about 530 deg over a full run, and wheel slip makes the odometry heading drift
       over that. That is why the first grasp was clean while later ones were
       millimetres off, and why objects were placed short of their slot. The LiDAR sits
       at z = 0.68 and the objects are at most 0.19 m high on the shelf, so these walls are the only
       thing it can see: fitting a line to the returns gives heading absolutely, with no
       dependence on how far the robot has turned. Flat rather than curved because a
       line fit uses every beam and tolerates the mast occluding part of it. -->
  <model name="shelf">
    <static>true</static>
    <link name="link">
      <!-- ============ slot 1: bottle, azimuth 130 deg ============ -->
      <collision name="ped1"><pose>-0.1928 0.2298 0.0500 0 0 2.2689</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.18 0.32 0.10</size></box></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>1.0</mu><mu2>1.0</mu2></ode></friction></surface></collision>
      <visual name="ped1v"><pose>-0.1928 0.2298 0.0500 0 0 2.2689</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.18 0.32 0.10</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.72 0.72 0.70 1</ambient><diffuse>0.72 0.72 0.70 1</diffuse></material></visual>
      <!-- chamfered barriers, outer then inner. Low friction so the object slides in. -->
      <collision name="ramp1o"><pose>-0.2391 0.2849 0.1047 0 -0.6109 2.2689</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.260 0.006</size></box></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>0.30</mu><mu2>0.30</mu2></ode></friction></surface></collision>
      <visual name="ramp1ov"><pose>-0.2391 0.2849 0.1047 0 -0.6109 2.2689</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.260 0.006</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.55 0.58 0.62 1</ambient><diffuse>0.55 0.58 0.62 1</diffuse></material></visual>
      <collision name="ramp1i"><pose>-0.1466 0.1747 0.1047 0 0.6109 2.2689</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.260 0.006</size></box></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>0.30</mu><mu2>0.30</mu2></ode></friction></surface></collision>
      <visual name="ramp1iv"><pose>-0.1466 0.1747 0.1047 0 0.6109 2.2689</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.260 0.006</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.55 0.58 0.62 1</ambient><diffuse>0.55 0.58 0.62 1</diffuse></material></visual>

      <!-- ============ slot 2: can, azimuth 180 deg ============ -->
      <collision name="ped2"><pose>-0.3000 0.0000 0.0500 0 0 3.1416</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.18 0.32 0.10</size></box></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>1.0</mu><mu2>1.0</mu2></ode></friction></surface></collision>
      <visual name="ped2v"><pose>-0.3000 0.0000 0.0500 0 0 3.1416</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.18 0.32 0.10</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.72 0.72 0.70 1</ambient><diffuse>0.72 0.72 0.70 1</diffuse></material></visual>
      <collision name="ramp2o"><pose>-0.3720 0.0000 0.1047 0 -0.6109 3.1416</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.260 0.006</size></box></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>0.30</mu><mu2>0.30</mu2></ode></friction></surface></collision>
      <visual name="ramp2ov"><pose>-0.3720 0.0000 0.1047 0 -0.6109 3.1416</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.260 0.006</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.55 0.58 0.62 1</ambient><diffuse>0.55 0.58 0.62 1</diffuse></material></visual>
      <collision name="ramp2i"><pose>-0.2280 0.0000 0.1047 0 0.6109 3.1416</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.260 0.006</size></box></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>0.30</mu><mu2>0.30</mu2></ode></friction></surface></collision>
      <visual name="ramp2iv"><pose>-0.2280 0.0000 0.1047 0 0.6109 3.1416</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.260 0.006</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.55 0.58 0.62 1</ambient><diffuse>0.55 0.58 0.62 1</diffuse></material></visual>

      <!-- ============ slot 3: box, azimuth 230 deg ============ -->
      <collision name="ped3"><pose>-0.1928 -0.2298 0.0500 0 0 4.0143</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.18 0.32 0.10</size></box></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>1.0</mu><mu2>1.0</mu2></ode></friction></surface></collision>
      <visual name="ped3v"><pose>-0.1928 -0.2298 0.0500 0 0 4.0143</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.18 0.32 0.10</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.72 0.72 0.70 1</ambient><diffuse>0.72 0.72 0.70 1</diffuse></material></visual>
      <collision name="ramp3o"><pose>-0.2391 -0.2849 0.1047 0 -0.6109 4.0143</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.260 0.006</size></box></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>0.30</mu><mu2>0.30</mu2></ode></friction></surface></collision>
      <visual name="ramp3ov"><pose>-0.2391 -0.2849 0.1047 0 -0.6109 4.0143</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.260 0.006</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.55 0.58 0.62 1</ambient><diffuse>0.55 0.58 0.62 1</diffuse></material></visual>
      <collision name="ramp3i"><pose>-0.1466 -0.1747 0.1047 0 0.6109 4.0143</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.260 0.006</size></box></geometry>
        <surface><friction><ode><mu>0.30</mu><mu2>0.30</mu2></ode></friction></surface></collision>
      <visual name="ramp3iv"><pose>-0.1466 -0.1747 0.1047 0 0.6109 4.0143</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.025 0.260 0.006</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.55 0.58 0.62 1</ambient><diffuse>0.55 0.58 0.62 1</diffuse></material></visual>

      <!-- ============ walls: the LiDAR pose reference ============ -->
      <!-- WALL HEIGHT 0.90, was 0.60. The LiDAR moved up to z=0.68 to get clear of the
           robot's own arms (which reach 0.610 and were being scanned as if they were
           wall), so the walls have to be taller than the sensor or it sees nothing at
           all. 0.90 leaves 0.22 m above the beam. -->
          <collision name="backwall"><pose>-0.46 0 0.45 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.02 0.90 0.90</size></box></geometry></collision>
      <visual name="backwallv"><pose>-0.46 0 0.45 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.02 0.90 0.90</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.62 0.62 0.60 1</ambient><diffuse>0.62 0.62 0.60 1</diffuse></material></visual>
      <!-- WALL B, perpendicular to A, forming an L-corner.
           One flat wall fixes heading and the distance to that wall, but NOT the
           coordinate parallel to it - sliding along a wall changes nothing about how
           it looks. Correcting heading alone was not enough: the base is only ever
           commanded to rotate, so the code assumed it stayed at the world origin, but
           the wheels slip (a run accumulated 29 deg of odometry heading error) and
           slipping wheels translate the base too. The robot walks off the origin, and
           then a grasp aimed at "0.30 m straight ahead" lands centimetres from the
           object and a placement lands off the pedestal. A second, perpendicular wall
           makes the full pose observable. -->
      <collision name="sidewall"><pose>-0.18 -0.46 0.45 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.56 0.02 0.90</size></box></geometry></collision>
      <visual name="sidewallv"><pose>-0.18 -0.46 0.45 0 0 0</pose>
        <geometry><box><size>0.56 0.02 0.90</size></box></geometry>
        <material><ambient>0.62 0.62 0.60 1</ambient><diffuse>0.62 0.62 0.60 1</diffuse></material></visual>
    </link>
  </model>
</sdf>
